  "_commit": {
    "id": "c4b207c593e7d2cbf02c3cde331693e2f16ef864",
    "author": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "committer": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "message": "Treat a stored NaN as missing in the margin functions, as base R does\n\nrowSums, colSums, rowMeans, colMeans, rowMaxs, colMaxs, rowVars,\ncolVars, rowSds and colSds kept a stored NaN with na.rm = TRUE, since SQL\naggregates skip NULL but not NaN.\n\n- With na.rm = TRUE, make a stored NaN NULL (.marginData) so the handling\n  of a stored NA applies to it. Only a floating point column can hold a\n  NaN, so other types are left alone. The test is dbplyr's is.nan(), a\n  printf() comparison; DuckDB's isnan() is not used because in DuckDB\n  2.0.0 it gives FALSE for a stored NaN in a Parquet column that has no\n  NULL, apparently because the file statistics leave NaN out.\n- With na.rm = FALSE, a variance or standard deviation of a margin with a\n  NaN is NA, as matrixStats gives, and a minimum is NaN: DuckDB orders\n  NaN above every number, so the minimum used to skip it.\n- With na.rm = TRUE, a maximum or minimum of a margin with no value left\n  is -Inf or Inf, as in base R, where it was NA. This predates the NaN\n  work.\n\nNot matched: a margin that holds both NA and NaN, summed or averaged with\nna.rm = FALSE. Base R gives NA or NaN depending on the order of the\nvalues; this gives NA.\n\nTests compare the twelve margin functions with matrixStats on matrices\nholding stored NaN and NA, with and without na.rm, by row and by column,\nand check the maximum and minimum of a margin with no value left. They\nfail on the previous code (79 failures). A random oracle gave 1440 cases\nwith NaN only and 0 mismatches. Suite: 4348 expectations, 0 failures at 1\nand 8 threads; BiocDuckDB 2201 expectations, 0 failures.\n",
    "time": 1795296645
  },
